{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 238 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0e8caba5343e9948080c4eafad0e98609ece6a42b70c3e439101a5b82b2a3c16" :edb/db-before-t 237 :edb/db-after-t 238 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533550 50 #inst "2026-10-11T06:07:35.971Z" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186151961 1071 "Let $a=85$ and $n=8$. Find positive integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a = qn+r$, where $0 \\leq r < n$, and determine $q \\cdot r$." 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186151961 1071 "Let $a=85$ and $n=8.$ Find positive integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a = qn+r,$ where $0 \\leq r <          n,$ and determine $q \\cdot r.$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186151961 1074 "Dividing $85$ by $8$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{10}} <     85\\div 8 <     11.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q={\\color{red}{10}}.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r = a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr = 85 - (10)(8) = {\\color{blue}{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $85 = qn + r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}85={{10}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 8+{{5}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q \\cdot r = 10 \\cdot 5 = 50.$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186151961 1074 "Dividing $85$ by $8$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{10}} < 85\\div 8 < 11\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So\n\n$$\nq={\\color{red}{10}}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r = a-qn$. This gives\n\n$$\nr = 85 - (10)(8) = {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n85 = qn + r\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n85 = 10_(⏟)_(q) \\cdot 8 + 5_(⏟)_(r)\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nq \\cdot r = 10 \\cdot 5 = 50\n$$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186151962 1071 "Let $a=-44$ and $n=6$. Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a = qn+r$, where $0 \\leq r < n$, and determine $q\\cdot r$." 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186151962 1071 "Let $a=-44$ and $n=6.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a = qn+r,$ where $0 \\leq r <         n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186151962 1074 "Dividing $-44$ by $6$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-8}} <      -44\\div 6 <      -7.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q={\\color{red}{-8}}.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r = a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr = -44 - (-8)(6) = {\\color{blue}{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-44 = qn + r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-44={{(-8)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 6+{{4}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r = -8 \\cdot 4 = -32.$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186151962 1074 "Dividing $-44$ by $6$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-8}} < -44\\div 6 < -7\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So\n\n$$\nq={\\color{red}{-8}}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r = a-qn$. This gives\n\n$$\nr = -44 - (-8)(6) = {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n-44 = qn + r\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n-44 = (-8)_(⏟)_(q) \\cdot 6 + 4_(⏟)_(r)\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nq\\cdot r = -8 \\cdot 4 = -32\n$$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186152170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407769 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407771 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407772 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407775 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407770 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407773 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407774 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407776 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152353 1050 420.0 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151073 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186152766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407761 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152766 1071 "Let $a = 47$ and $n = 7$. Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a = qn + r$, where $0 \\le r < n$. Then, $r =$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186152766 1071 "Let $a=47$ and $n=7.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n.$ Then, $r=$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152766 1074 "Dividing $47$ by $7$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n6<47÷7<7.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=6.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=47-(6)(7)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $47=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}47={{6}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 7+{{5}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r=5.$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151085 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186152768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407762 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152768 1071 "Let $a =-50$ and $n = 7$. Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a = qn + r$, where $0 \\le r < n$, and determine $q \\cdot r$." 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186152768 1071 "Let $a=-50$ and $n=7.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186152768 1074 "Dividing $-50$ by $7$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n-8<-50÷7<-7.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=-8.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=-50-(-8)(7)=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-50=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50={{(-8)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 7+{{6}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r=-8\\cdot 6=-48.$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186153365 1064 "Suppose that $a$ is an integer and that $n$ is a positive integer. Then there are *unique* integers $q$ (called the **quotient**) and $r$ (called the **remainder**) such that\n\n$$\na = qn + r \\quad\\textrm{where}\\quad 0 \\leq r \\lt n\n$$\n\nThis simple idea has far-reaching consequences, as we'll soon discover.\n\nAs an example, suppose that $a=56$ and $n=5$. Then, we can write\n\n$$\n56 = \\underbrace{{\\color{red}{11}}}_{q}\\cdot 5 + \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{1}}}_{r}\n$$\n\nWe can find $q$ using integer division. Once we know $q$, it's then straightforward to find $r$." 13194139533550 17592186045585 false] [17592186153365 1064 "Suppose that $a$ is an integer and that $n$ is a positive integer. Then there are *unique* integers $q$ (called the **quotient**) and $r$ (called the **remainder**) such that\n\n$$\na = qn + r \\quad\\textrm{where}\\quad 0 \\leq r \\lt n.\n$$\n\nThis simple idea has far-reaching consequences, as we'll soon discover. \n\nAs an example, suppose that $a=56$ and $n=5.$ Then, we can write \n\n$$\n56 = \\underbrace{{\\color{red}{11}}}_{q}\\cdot 5 + \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{1}}}_{r}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $q$ using integer division. Once we know $q,$ it's then straightforward to find $r.$" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407709 1081 "8" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407709 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407709 1084 #uuid "025eeed5-68d4-5570-b021-0adf7e52b725" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407710 1081 "-7" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407710 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407710 1084 #uuid "092038e3-d8b5-5a85-ac6f-ac53305df0d5" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407711 1081 "4" 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407711 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533550 17592186045585 true] [17592186407711 1084 #uuid "108a39ab-f87b-50a6-bd2a-df176eaa91f8" 13194139533